# Q29 verification pin — a BORROWED `&String` (owned by the caller) passed into
# a `lib "C"` FFI function must forward the correct data POINTER and the correct
# LENGTH.
#
# Ruxen's String ABI is a bare NUL-terminated `char*` (no length header;
# `library/std/string/runtime/string.c`), so the C side recovers length via
# `strlen`. `include?`/`starts_with`/`find`/`replace`/`split` all take a
# `needle: &String` and run pointer/length-sensitive byte ops (`strstr`,
# `strncmp`) on it. If `&String` forwarded the wrong pointer or a char-count
# instead of the data (the OLD ledger claim that forced canvas's
# `measure_text_n_raw(n: Int)` fallback), these would mismatch. They don't — the
# borrowed `&String` carries the real bytes + length across the boundary.
#
# The locals + needle arguments are BARE string literals (no `String.from`): a
# bare literal is an owned `String`, and coerces to the `&String` needle at the
# call site — which is exactly the borrowed-`&String` path under test.
def main
  let s = "hello, sub-pixel world"
  let needle = "sub-pixel"     # borrowed into include?/find/replace
  puts "len=#{s.size}"
  puts "has=#{s.include?(needle)}"           # strstr over the borrowed needle
  puts "starts=#{s.starts_with("hello")}"
  puts "missing=#{s.include?("zzz")}"
  match s.find(needle)
    Some(i) -> puts "at=#{i}"                # byte index — length/offset sensitive
    nil     -> puts "at=none"
  end
  let r = s.replace(needle, "WHOLE")
  puts "rep=#{r}"
end
